2. What you may do

You may, free of charge:

  (a) read, run and inspect the Software on your own device, to look at how it
      works, to evaluate it or to learn from reading it;
  (b) report bugs, security problems and ideas to the Copyright Holder;
  (c) fork the repository on GitHub, and only on GitHub, to read it there and
      to send a pull request back to this repository. This is what GitHub's
      Terms of Service already allow for any public repository, and it is all
      this license adds to them.

3. What you may not do

Without the prior written permission of the Copyright Holder, you may not:

  (a) copy, redistribute, publish or sublicense the Software or any part of it,
      except as section 2(c) allows;
  (b) host, deploy or offer the Software or a build of it to anyone, as a
      website, a server, a download or in any other way;
  (c) modify it or make derivative works from it, except for a pull request
      under section 2(c);
  (d) keep a fork for your own use, or use it for independent development;
  (e) take code, shaders, models, textures, level data, UI or any other part of
      the Software and use it in another project, commercial or not.

